title: Collator protocol — honor the scheduling lookahead, not a claim-queue/ancestry length

doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    Both validator sides of the collator protocol derived the claim-queue window length from a
    value that is only a *lower bound* on the runtime scheduling lookahead, and so truncated the
    tail of the claim queue — silently dropping advertisement positions that are in fact
    fetchable. Each side used a different bound, and each falls short in a different situation:

    * Experimental (`validator_side_experimental`) used the allowed-ancestry path length. This is
      below the lookahead near genesis, and for the first `lookahead - 1` blocks after a
      **session change**, where the implicit view clamps ancestry at the session boundary (the
      relay chain cannot back candidates from a previous session).
    * Legacy (`validator_side`, `is_slot_available`) used the claim-queue length. The runtime
      returns a claim queue shorter than the lookahead when it cannot predict an assignment for
      every position — principally an **under-scheduled on-demand core**, where fewer orders are
      queued than there are positions to fill, and more generally any core left unscheduled at
      some position within the lookahead.

    In those windows, advertisements landing on the tail claim-queue positions — reachable from
    the scheduling parents on the path, including the leaf itself — were rejected, reducing
    collation throughput.

    Fix: fetch the scheduling lookahead from the runtime (`SchedulingLookahead`) per active leaf
    and use it as the window bound in both validator sides. The per-leaf claim queues and the
    lookahead are now carried together in a shared `LeafClaimQueues` type, fetched in one place
    so the lookahead can never again be inferred from a length.

    No public API changes; behavior change is confined to the collator-protocol validator sides.

crates:
- name: polkadot-collator-protocol
  bump: patch
